

UNIX(4)              UNIX Programmer's Manual               UNIX(4)



NAME
     unix - UNIX-domain protocol family

SYNOPSIS
     #include <sys/types.h>
     #include <sys/socket.h>
     #include <sys/un.h>

     s = socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);

DESCRIPTION
     The UNIX-domain protocol family delivers datagrams between
     sockets on one machine.  It uses the filesystem name space
     for addresses and no network of any kind: there is no
     protocol below it, nothing is serialised onto a wire, and a
     machine with no Ethernet card has the whole family available.

     Only SOCK_DGRAM is provided.  Upstream also offers
     SOCK_STREAM, which is what pipe(2) is built from on those
     systems; this kernel has real pipes, so a request for
     SOCK_STREAM is refused with EPROTOTYPE rather than answered
     with a descriptor that has no transport behind it.

ADDRESSING
     A UNIX-domain address is a pathname, carried in a
     sockaddr_un:

          struct sockaddr_un {
                  short   sun_family;     /* AF_UNIX */
                  char    sun_path[108];
          };

     sun_path IS 108 BYTES AND A sockaddr IS 16 IN TOTAL, so an
     address buffer sized as a sockaddr truncates every path past
     the thirteenth character -- and bind(2) then names a
     different, shorter file and succeeds, so the caller learns
     nothing until a later stat(2) of the name it meant answers
     ENOENT.  Declare a sockaddr_un, or size the buffer SUN_LEN.

     bind(2) creates a socket node on the filesystem, of type
     VSOCK: stat(2) reports it through S_ISSOCK and ls(1) shows it
     as a socket.  connect(2) resolves a path to the socket bound
     to it and requires write permission on the node, which is
     what makes chmod(1) on the name mean something.

     A BOUND SOCKET HOLDS ITS NODE'S VNODE for as long as it is
     open, and close(2) releases it.  close(2) does NOT remove the
     name -- a daemon that wants to rebind should unlink(2) first,
     which is what syslogd does -- so a socket node outliving its
     socket is the ordinary case after a restart.  Connecting to
     such a name answers ECONNREFUSED.

     The receive queue belongs to the BOUND socket rather than to
     a connection, so one queue serves every client that has
     connected to a name, however many there are.  A client end
     holds no queue of its own.

DIAGNOSTICS
     A socket(2) for SOCK_STREAM or SOCK_RAW answers EPROTOTYPE.
     SOCK_SEQPACKET is not refused because it does not exist:
     <sys/socket.h> defines only the three types above.

     A bind(2) of a name that already exists answers EADDRINUSE;
     upstream marks adopting the existing node as a FIFO-style
     improvement it did not make, and neither does this.

     A connect(2) to a name that is not a socket answers ENOTSOCK,
     to a socket node whose socket has closed ECONNREFUSED, and to
     one now held by another protocol EPROTOTYPE.

     A send(2) of a datagram larger than the receive queue can
     ever hold answers EMSGSIZE, decided before the transport is
     entered.  ENOBUFS means the queue has no room AT THIS MOMENT
     and is worth retrying; EMSGSIZE never is.

SEE ALSO
     socket(2), bind(2), connect(2), send(2), recv(2), unlink(2),
     syslog(3), pipe(2)

BUGS
     NO ACCESS RIGHTS.  Upstream passes descriptors between
     processes over a UNIX-domain socket, as ancillary data with
     an SCM_RIGHTS control message.  Nothing of that is here:
     sendmsg(2) and recvmsg(2) do not exist, there is no msghdr,
     and the garbage collector upstream needs for descriptors in
     flight has nothing to collect.  A port that expects to hand a
     descriptor to another process cannot do it this way.

     EVERY DATAGRAM ARRIVES FROM AN UNNAMED SENDER.  Upstream
     attaches the sender's bound address to each record, so
     recvfrom(2) can answer who sent it; here a sender's name is
     not carried, and recvfrom(2) answers with upstream's own
     sun_noname: a 16-byte sockaddr whose family is AF_UNIX and
     whose path is empty.  That is what upstream gives for a
     sender that never bound, so a caller written against
     upstream reads a legitimate answer -- it just always reads
     that one.  A protocol that identifies its peers by their
     bound names will not work.

     SENDTO(2) DOES NOT NAME A DESTINATION.  Upstream lets a
     datagram socket give a path per call: an unconnected socket
     is connected for that one datagram, and a connected one
     answers EISCONN.  Here an AF_UNIX socket reaches a peer only
     through connect(2), and sendto(2) on one answers EINVAL
     whatever it names; send(2) on the connected socket is the
     form that works.

     TRUNCATION IS SILENT.  A recv(2) whose buffer is smaller than
     the datagram returns as many bytes as fit and discards the
     remainder, which is upstream's behaviour; but MSG_TRUNC is
     not reported in the flags, so a caller cannot tell a message
     that exactly filled its buffer from one that was cut.  Size
     the buffer, or compare against what the sender promised.

     THE QUEUE CEILING IS A GROWN RING AND NOT AN MBUF BUDGET.
     Upstream bounds a receive queue by sb_hiwat against a shared
     mbuf pool, so pressure elsewhere in the system shows up here;
     this queue is a pipe ring that grows to 4096 bytes and is
     bounded by that alone.  The observable difference is that
     EMSGSIZE arrives at a fixed size rather than a varying one.

     The number of simultaneously bound sockets is bounded by the
     machine's pipe count, which is shared with pipe(2) -- so
     enough bound sockets will stop pipelines working in a shell.
     Upstream has no equivalent coupling.

HISTORY
     A UNIX-domain protocol family appeared in 4.2BSD.  This one
     provides the SOCK_DGRAM half of it, added so that syslog(3)
     would have a deterministic sink.
